TECHNICAL OFFICIALS

Become a Technical Official

The Obstacle Course Racing Association of Ireland (OCRA ÉIREANN) is developing a national pathway for Technical Officials within obstacle sport.

Technical Officials, commonly referred to as TOs, play a critical role in competitive OCR.

They help ensure that competitions are delivered fairly, consistently and in accordance with the applicable sporting rules.

A strong Technical Official programme is essential as competitive obstacle sport continues to grow in Ireland.


What Does a Technical Official Do?

Technical Officials are responsible for observing competition and applying the rules of the event.

Depending on their role and level of experience, responsibilities may include:

- Monitoring obstacle completion
- Applying competition rules
- Recording obstacle failures or penalties
- Monitoring athlete lanes and course boundaries
- Supporting start and finish procedures
- Checking competition equipment or course areas
- Recording incidents
- Supporting timing and results processes
- Responding to athlete questions
- Assisting with protests and reviews
- Supervising other Technical Officials
- Supporting competition management

Technical Officials must remain impartial and apply the same standards to every athlete.


More Than Watching Obstacles

Technical officiating involves considerably more than standing beside an obstacle and deciding whether somebody touched a bell.

Officials need to understand:

- The competition rules
- Correct obstacle completion
- Athlete safety
- Communication
- Positioning and observation
- Incident reporting
- Penalty procedures
- Competition flow
- Athlete conduct
- Protest procedures
- Their own authority and responsibilities

More senior officials may also need to understand course operations, team management, competition schedules and the wider technical delivery of an event.


The Technical Officials Pathway

OCRA is developing a progressive education pathway that will allow officials to build their knowledge and experience over time.

The pathway will begin with entry-level officiating and progress towards roles involving greater technical and competition responsibility.

Development will combine:

- Rules education
- Practical training
- Event experience
- Mentoring
- Assessment
- Continuing development

The detailed Technical Official levels and certification requirements will be published as the pathway is finalised.


Starting as a Technical Official

Entry-level Technical Official education will focus on the knowledge required to support competition safely and consistently.

This may include:

- Introduction to obstacle sport
- Role of the Technical Official
- Basic competition rules
- Obstacle completion
- Penalties
- Athlete safety
- Observation and positioning
- Communication
- Recording decisions
- Professional conduct

New officials should then gain practical experience at suitable events alongside more experienced officials.


Developing as an Official

With additional training and practical experience, Technical Officials may progress towards responsibilities such as:

- Leading an obstacle team
- Supervising sections of a course
- Managing start or finish operations
- Supporting results verification
- Reviewing incidents
- Supporting protest processes
- Briefing officials
- Managing Technical Official teams
- Supporting National Championship delivery
- Working at international competitions


Fairness and Impartiality

Technical Officials must apply competition rules consistently regardless of:

- Athlete
- Club
- Nationality
- Ability
- Reputation
- Competitive position

Officials should avoid conflicts of interest and should raise potential conflicts before competition wherever possible.

Trust in competition depends on athletes knowing that the same rules are being applied to everyone.


Technical Officials and Athlete Safety

Technical Officials are not substitutes for event safety personnel, medical teams or event management.

However, officials are often positioned close to athletes and obstacles and therefore have an important role in identifying immediate issues and following established safety procedures.

Officials must understand when competition should continue, when an issue should be escalated and who has authority to make safety-related decisions.


Rules Education

Obstacle sport contains multiple disciplines and competition formats.

Officials may therefore require discipline-specific education for events such as:

- 100m
- 400m
- Short Course
- Standard Course
- Team and Relay competition
- Other recognised formats

Technical Officials are expected to remain familiar with the current version of the rules applying to the competition they are officiating.


Practical Experience

Technical officiating is a practical role.

Education should therefore combine classroom or online learning with experience at real competitions.

Officials will be encouraged to build experience across different:

- Obstacles
- Race formats
- Athlete categories
- Competition levels
- Operational roles

Practical experience will form an important part of progression through the OCRA Technical Officials pathway.


Continuing Development

Rules and competition formats evolve.

Technical Officials should continue developing through:

- Event experience
- Rules updates
- Refresher training
- Workshops
- Mentoring
- Competition reviews
- Advanced technical training

Experienced officials may also support the development and mentoring of newer Technical Officials.


Who Can Become a Technical Official?

You do not need to be an elite OCR athlete to become a Technical Official.

The role may suit:

- Current or former OCR athletes
- Coaches
- Club members
- Volunteers
- Race organisers
- People with officiating experience from other sports
- Anyone interested in the technical side of competition

A good Technical Official needs attention to detail, fairness, confidence, communication skills and a willingness to learn.


Why Become a Technical Official?

Becoming a TO provides another way to contribute to obstacle sport beyond competing.

It can provide opportunities to:

- Support local events
- Help develop Irish competition
- Learn the sport in greater technical detail
- Work with athletes and race organisers
- Officiate National Championships
- Progress towards higher-level competition
- Support Team Ireland and international events
- Become part of the national Technical Official community


Interested in Becoming a TO?

OCRA welcomes people interested in learning how to officiate obstacle sport.

Current education and training opportunities will be published within the Courses & Training section.


